+</div><p>The important idea is that Spark is not “one big program running faster.” It divides work into many independent tasks that can run in parallel across a cluster.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Explain the difference between an application, driver, executor and task in one clean flow.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s1"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Driver: the brain of the application</h2><p>The driver is the process running your main Spark application. In PySpark, your Python code communicates with Spark and the driver coordinates execution.</p><ul><li>Creates the SparkSession / SparkContext.</li><li>Builds the logical lineage and DAG from your transformations.</li><li>Creates jobs when an action is called.</li><li>Splits work into stages and tasks.</li><li>Schedules tasks on executors and tracks progress.</li><li>Collects results or coordinates writing output.</li></ul><p>The driver normally does <em>coordination</em>; executors do the distributed computation.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>A common mistake is saying the driver processes all the data. Explain that it coordinates distributed work and can become a bottleneck if you collect too much data to it.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s2"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>SparkSession and SparkContext</h2><p><strong>SparkSession</strong> is the modern high-level entry point for DataFrame, SQL and many Spark operations. <strong>SparkContext</strong> is the lower-level context that represents the connection to the Spark cluster and coordinates core execution.</p><p>In typical PySpark code, you work with <code>spark</code>, and the underlying SparkContext is available as <code>spark.sparkContext</code>.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Know the simple answer: SparkSession is the modern unified entry point; SparkContext is the lower-level core context used for cluster coordination.</p><pre><code>from pyspark.sql import SparkSession
 
 spark = SparkSession.builder     .appName("InterviewReady")     .getOrCreate()
 
 sc = spark.sparkContext</code></pre></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s3"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Cluster manager: the resource allocator</h2><p>The cluster manager is responsible for allocating resources for Spark applications. Spark can run with its own standalone manager or on resource managers such as YARN or Kubernetes.</p><p>The cluster manager does not replace Spark's internal execution scheduling. It allocates the resources; once executors are available, Spark's driver schedules application tasks onto them.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Clearly separate <strong>resource allocation</strong> from <strong>task scheduling</strong>. Cluster manager allocates executors/resources; Spark schedules tasks to those executors.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s4"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Worker nodes and executors</h2><p>A worker node is a machine in the cluster that can provide compute resources. An executor is a process launched for a specific Spark application and runs tasks assigned by the driver.</p><ul><li>Executes tasks.</li><li>Stores cached or persisted partitions when requested.</li><li>Reports task status/results to the driver.</li><li>Lives for the application unless dynamic allocation changes executor availability.</li></ul><p>Executors from one application are logically separate from executors of another application.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Worker is a machine/resource host. Executor is the application-specific process that actually runs Spark tasks.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s5"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Tasks: the unit of parallel work</h2><p>A task is the smallest unit of work that Spark schedules. Within a stage, Spark creates tasks based largely on the partitions that need processing.</p><p>A useful interview mental model is: <strong>one task processes one partition at a time</strong>. More partitions can create more potential parallelism, but excessive tiny tasks create scheduling overhead.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Connect partitions to tasks: the number of tasks in a stage is closely tied to the number of partitions being processed or produced.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s6"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>From code to execution: DAG → Job → Stage → Task</h2><p>Spark transformations are generally lazy. Calling transformations builds a lineage/DAG rather than immediately processing data. An action such as <code>count()</code>, <code>show()</code> or <code>write</code> triggers execution.</p><ol><li><strong>DAG:</strong> graph of transformations and dependencies.</li><li><strong>Job:</strong> execution triggered by an action.</li><li><strong>Stage:</strong> a set of tasks that can execute without requiring a shuffle boundary.</li><li><strong>Task:</strong> the individual unit scheduled to an executor.</li></ol><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Be precise: an action triggers a job; shuffle/wide dependencies typically create stage boundaries; partitions drive task parallelism.</p><pre><code>df = spark.read.parquet("/data")
 result = df.filter("amount > 100")            .groupBy("country")            .count()
 
-result.show()   # action → job → stages → tasks</code></pre></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s7"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>DAG Scheduler and Task Scheduler</h2><p>The <strong>DAG Scheduler</strong> understands the lineage and shuffle dependencies. It divides a job into stages and determines the dependency structure.</p><p>The <strong>Task Scheduler</strong> is responsible for launching individual tasks for those stages on available executor resources and handling task-level scheduling concerns.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>A simple distinction: DAG Scheduler thinks in stages and dependencies; Task Scheduler thinks in individual tasks and executor slots.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s8"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Narrow vs wide dependencies</h2><p>A <strong>narrow dependency</strong> means an output partition depends on a small, predictable set of input partitions, so work can usually continue in the same stage.</p><p>A <strong>wide dependency</strong> means output partitions depend on many input partitions. This requires data redistribution—a shuffle—and commonly creates a stage boundary.</p><p>Examples often discussed: <code>map</code> and <code>filter</code> are narrow; <code>groupByKey</code>, many joins and <code>repartition</code> can require wide dependencies.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Do not define wide transformation merely as “expensive.” The defining idea is the dependency/data redistribution pattern; the expense usually comes from the resulting shuffle.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s9"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Shuffle: the network-heavy boundary</h2><p>During a shuffle, Spark redistributes data so records with the same required partitioning relationship end up together. This can involve serialization, disk I/O, network transfer, sorting, memory pressure and spill.</p><p>That is why shuffles are often among the first things to inspect when a Spark workload is slow.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>When optimizing, ask: can I avoid the shuffle, reduce shuffled data, improve partitioning, or use a different join/aggregation strategy?</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s10"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>How a Spark application starts</h2><ol><li>You submit an application.</li><li>The driver starts in client or cluster deployment mode.</li><li>The driver connects to the cluster manager.</li><li>Resources are allocated and executors are launched.</li><li>The driver sends application code and tasks to executors.</li><li>Transformations build lineage; actions trigger jobs.</li><li>Tasks run, results are returned or output is written.</li></ol><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Tell the lifecycle as a story. Interviewers often test whether you can connect the architecture components instead of defining them separately.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s11"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Client mode vs cluster mode</h2><p>These terms describe where the driver runs. In <strong>client mode</strong>, the driver runs outside the cluster (for example, on the machine from which the application is submitted). In <strong>cluster mode</strong>, the driver runs inside the cluster.</p><p>This matters for reliability, network access and what happens if the submitting client disconnects.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Do not confuse deploy mode with cluster manager. YARN/Kubernetes describes the resource-management environment; client/cluster describes driver placement.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s12"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Fault tolerance and lineage</h2><p>Spark can recompute lost data using lineage: the sequence of transformations used to derive a dataset. Spark's distributed execution also retries failed tasks according to configuration and deployment behavior.</p><p>Persisting data changes the trade-off: recomputation may be avoided when cached blocks are available, but caching consumes memory/storage resources.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Explain fault tolerance as recomputation from lineage plus task retry—not as “Spark magically stores every intermediate result in memory.”</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s13"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Data locality</h2><p>Spark prefers running work close to where data is available when possible. Better locality can reduce network transfer. Locality is one factor in scheduling; resource availability and other constraints also matter.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Mention locality when explaining why data distribution and cluster layout can affect performance.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s14"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Architecture misconceptions interviewers love</h2><div class="myth-grid"><div><strong>Myth:</strong> Driver executes all transformations.<br><span>Reality: executors run distributed tasks; the driver coordinates.</span></div><div><strong>Myth:</strong> One executor equals one task.<br><span>Reality: executors run many tasks over time using available cores/slots.</span></div><div><strong>Myth:</strong> Every transformation creates a stage.<br><span>Reality: stage boundaries are strongly associated with shuffle/wide dependencies.</span></div><div><strong>Myth:</strong> More partitions always means faster.<br><span>Reality: too few limits parallelism; too many adds scheduling and small-task overhead.</span></div></div><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Use these contrasts in interviews to demonstrate that you understand the execution model rather than just terminology.</p></article>
+result.show()   # action → job → stages → tasks</code></pre><div class="svg-diagram-wrap">
