+</div></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s7"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>DAG Scheduler and Task Scheduler</h2><p>The <strong>DAG Scheduler</strong> understands the lineage and shuffle dependencies. It divides a job into stages and determines the dependency structure.</p><p>The <strong>Task Scheduler</strong> is responsible for launching individual tasks for those stages on available executor resources and handling task-level scheduling concerns.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>A simple distinction: DAG Scheduler thinks in stages and dependencies; Task Scheduler thinks in individual tasks and executor slots.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s8"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Narrow vs wide dependencies</h2><p>A <strong>narrow dependency</strong> means an output partition depends on a small, predictable set of input partitions, so work can usually continue in the same stage.</p><p>A <strong>wide dependency</strong> means output partitions depend on many input partitions. This requires data redistribution—a shuffle—and commonly creates a stage boundary.</p><p>Examples often discussed: <code>map</code> and <code>filter</code> are narrow; <code>groupByKey</code>, many joins and <code>repartition</code> can require wide dependencies.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Do not define wide transformation merely as “expensive.” The defining idea is the dependency/data redistribution pattern; the expense usually comes from the resulting shuffle.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s9"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Shuffle: the network-heavy boundary</h2><p>During a shuffle, Spark redistributes data so records with the same required partitioning relationship end up together. This can involve serialization, disk I/O, network transfer, sorting, memory pressure and spill.</p><p>That is why shuffles are often among the first things to inspect when a Spark workload is slow.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>When optimizing, ask: can I avoid the shuffle, reduce shuffled data, improve partitioning, or use a different join/aggregation strategy?</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s10"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>How a Spark application starts</h2><ol><li>You submit an application.</li><li>The driver starts in client or cluster deployment mode.</li><li>The driver connects to the cluster manager.</li><li>Resources are allocated and executors are launched.</li><li>The driver sends application code and tasks to executors.</li><li>Transformations build lineage; actions trigger jobs.</li><li>Tasks run, results are returned or output is written.</li></ol><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Tell the lifecycle as a story. Interviewers often test whether you can connect the architecture components instead of defining them separately.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s11"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Client mode vs cluster mode</h2><p>These terms describe where the driver runs. In <strong>client mode</strong>, the driver runs outside the cluster (for example, on the machine from which the application is submitted). In <strong>cluster mode</strong>, the driver runs inside the cluster.</p><p>This matters for reliability, network access and what happens if the submitting client disconnects.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Do not confuse deploy mode with cluster manager. YARN/Kubernetes describes the resource-management environment; client/cluster describes driver placement.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s12"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Fault tolerance and lineage</h2><p>Spark can recompute lost data using lineage: the sequence of transformations used to derive a dataset. Spark's distributed execution also retries failed tasks according to configuration and deployment behavior.</p><p>Persisting data changes the trade-off: recomputation may be avoided when cached blocks are available, but caching consumes memory/storage resources.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Explain fault tolerance as recomputation from lineage plus task retry—not as “Spark magically stores every intermediate result in memory.”</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s13"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Data locality</h2><p>Spark prefers running work close to where data is available when possible. Better locality can reduce network transfer. Locality is one factor in scheduling; resource availability and other constraints also matter.</p><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Mention locality when explaining why data distribution and cluster layout can affect performance.</p></article><article class="lesson-card" id="s14"><span class="lesson-label">SPARK ARCHITECTURE</span><h2>Architecture misconceptions interviewers love</h2><div class="myth-grid"><div><strong>Myth:</strong> Driver executes all transformations.<br><span>Reality: executors run distributed tasks; the driver coordinates.</span></div><div><strong>Myth:</strong> One executor equals one task.<br><span>Reality: executors run many tasks over time using available cores/slots.</span></div><div><strong>Myth:</strong> Every transformation creates a stage.<br><span>Reality: stage boundaries are strongly associated with shuffle/wide dependencies.</span></div><div><strong>Myth:</strong> More partitions always means faster.<br><span>Reality: too few limits parallelism; too many adds scheduling and small-task overhead.</span></div></div><h4>Interview focus</h4><p>Use these contrasts in interviews to demonstrate that you understand the execution model rather than just terminology.</p></article>
 <section class="quick-practice architecture-qa"><h2>🔥 Architecture interview questions</h2>
 <div class="qa-list">
 <div class="qa"><h4>1. Explain Spark architecture in 60 seconds.</h4><p>A Spark application has a driver that coordinates execution. The driver requests resources from a cluster manager, which launches executors on worker nodes. Transformations build a DAG, an action triggers a job, the DAG is split into stages around shuffle boundaries, and each stage is divided into tasks that executors run in parallel.</p></div>
